> I know it just means that it's a carriage return..
> but... *shrug* other files within the nh++ source don't have it..
> *ponder*
> any answers? ;)

Yep... you've uploaded the files from a DOS archive.  DOS stores newlines as
CR & LF, which is why you've got some extra ^M characters in there.

Just run this (Bourne) shell script on any files which have ^M's in them:

  tr -d '\015' < $1 > $1.TMP
  mv $1.TMP $1

That'll strip out any ^M's.
 
